This directory contains the Packfall Python wrapper and a small set of
examples for the current shared-library workflow.

Build the shared library first:

  make -f Makefile.msys2 shlib

or on Linux:

  make -f Makefile.linux shlib

The canonical Python wrapper is:

  from packfall import Packfall

The lowercase alias remains available for compatibility with older scripts:

  from packfall import packfall

The wrapper looks for in-repo shared libraries first:

  build/msys2/libpackfall.dll
  build/linux/libpackfall.so

Examples in python/examples:

  demo.py
    exercises the wrapper API directly from Python

  gui.py
    Qt-based run/stop control with a live 2D atom view

  vizplotgui.py
    Qt-based live atom view plus a live compute plot

  fabric_tuner.py
    Tkinter-based parameter UI for ``mesh/surface/fabric`` conveyor-belt
    inputs. It loads the selected input through the shared library, lets you
    apply a new fabric session, run a chosen number of steps, and save a tuned
    input. It renders the live fabric mesh and DEM particles from isolated STL
    snapshots and library atom data; drag to orbit and use the mouse wheel to
    zoom. The head-pulley fabric example is used by default.

  in.gui
  in.viz
    input files for the GUI examples

Run examples from the repository root, for example:

  python python/examples/demo.py
  python python/examples/gui.py python/examples/in.gui 100
  python python/examples/vizplotgui.py python/examples/in.viz 100 thermo_temp
  python python/examples/fabric_tuner.py
  python python/examples/fabric_tuner.py examples/fabric_head_pulley/in.fabric_head_pulley

The fabric tuner changes its values when it recreates the fabric fix, so
``Apply / reload`` starts a fresh session at step zero. This is required by the
shared library's fabric implementation; ``Run one frame`` and ``Start live``
advance the library in preview-sized chunks. ``Stop`` takes effect between
chunks, and ``Save tuned input`` keeps the values in an ordinary Packfall input
file. Preview snapshots are written to a temporary directory, never to the
input's normal output location.

To install the wrapper into a Python environment, use python/install.py.
